WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.601
In Birdman, Edward Norton plays a selfish,
pretentious actor whom nobody wants to work

00:00:04.601 --> 00:00:08.340
with because he's so difficult...which allegedly
may not be too far from the truth.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:12.400
At least Norton has a sense of humor about
his reputation, but it hasn't helped his career

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.400
too much.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:15.870
Here's why Edward Norton doesn't get many
movie offers.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.019
The Incredible Hulk

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:22.180
In 2008, Marvel Studios approached Norton
to star in their Hulk reboot, but he initially

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:23.180
turned it down.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:28.359
After meeting with director Louis Leterrier,
Norton signed on, but with a ridiculous condition:

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:32.540
any suggestions he made to the screenplay
had to be incorporated into filming.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.950
Norton evidently did a substantial rewrite
of the movie just weeks before filming started.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:41.270
Leterrier shot as much of Norton's script
as possible, alongside the original script.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.010
The resulting mess was too convoluted for
theaters.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:48.000
Marvel executives hated the edit and ordered
a new one with more action.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.860
Norton more or less Hulked out in real life…

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.600
"Raaaaaggghhhhh!!!"

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.040
...and Marvel resented Norton's meddling so
much that Norton got the boot in favor of

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.550
Mark Ruffalo when it came time for The Avengers.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:04.480
Marvel took the rare step of issuing a statement
about why Norton was dropped, saying they

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.940
wanted "an actor who embodies the creativity
and collaborative spirit of our other talented

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:09.940
cast members."

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.900
In short: they were sick of him and didn't
like him when he was angry.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.190
Or at all.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.300
Red Dragon

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.970
The LA Times reported that in 2002, during
the filming of the Silence of the Lambs prequel,

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.350
Red Dragon, Norton showed up on set with brand-new,
totally unsolicited script pages that he'd

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:26.830
written for himself.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:30.920
Director Brett Ratner and his producers didn't
take kindly to Norton's demands to film the

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.800
scenes, and an argument ensued.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.440
It's just one of many instances where Norton
ignored the basic chain of command that makes

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.730
movie sets run smoothly.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.830
So, it's easy to see how Norton was beginning
to set up his career to crash and burn, even

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.110
back in 2002.

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.500
Frida

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:54.170
In the 2002 bio-pic about artist Frida Kahlo,
Norton played Nelson Rockefeller, the billionaire

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:58.840
who commissioned Kahlo's husband, Diego Rivera,
to paint a mural in New York's Rockefeller

00:01:58.840 --> 00:01:59.840
Center.

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.130
Norton's then-girlfriend, Salma Hayek, had
been trying to get the movie made for years.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.369
Wanting to get every detail perfect, she asked
Norton to conduct additional research and

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:13.010
use whatever he found to rewrite the Frida
screenplay that had already been completed.

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.920
Norton has since taken credit for the final
cut, but the Writer’s Guild refused to credit

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.700
him, since he violated their rules.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:23.200
Norton trashed the WGA in a 2007 interview
with The AV Club, saying,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.210
"I wrote Frida.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.740
I wasn't a member of the Writer's Guild, and
I didn't get paid.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.420
[...] I just got sort of shafted by them,
quite frankly."

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.090
Death to Smoochy

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:34.460
Not even the costume department is safe from
Norton's interference.

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.940
During production of the 2002 dark comedy
Death to Smoochy, the film's costume designer,

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.390
Jane Ruhm, presented Norton with a wide variety
of clothes for his character.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.310
But that wasn't good enough for Norton.

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:48.310
Without anyone's knowledge, Norton commissioned
Armani to design him the ultimate in hippie

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:50.350
chic: a suit made of actual hemp.

00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.800
Ruhm later told Premiere Magazine that Norton
made her deal with all the paperwork associated

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:58.890
with getting the suit made and sent to set,
rather than, you know, letting her do her

00:02:58.890 --> 00:02:59.990
job to begin with.

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:02.100
American History X

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.600
Norton was nominated for an Oscar for the
1998 drama American History X, but he didn't

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:10.670
just star in the movie: he also edited it,
not that anybody had asked him to.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.569
During the shoot, Norton and director Tony
Kaye reportedly clashed about Norton's character

00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:17.700
and dialogue, but things got really bad during
the editing phase.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:21.970
Kaye had created a tight 95-minute cut of
the film, but when Norton saw it, he thought

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.710
that Kaye had cut too much.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:27.569
Not wanting to upset their star, the production
company relented to Norton's demand that he

00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:31.600
make his own Norton-centric cut of the movie,
which clocked in at well over two hours.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:35.320
That's the version that made it to theaters,
which upset Kaye, prompting him to ask the

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.781
Directors Guild to remove his name from the
film's credits and replace it with "Humpty

00:03:38.781 --> 00:03:39.781
Dumpty."

00:03:39.781 --> 00:03:44.700
His request was denied, and his $200 million
lawsuit against the production didn't fly,

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:45.700
either.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.790
Kaye took out 40 ads in trade papers eviscerating
Norton, and told a reporter the actor was

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:52.580
"a narcissistic dilettante who raped the film."

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:53.580
Jeez...

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.280
It goes without saying that the two haven't
worked together again.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.280
"Jee wizz"

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.280
